mcprack for Debian
===================

## Installation

Run the package installer, which will prompt for configuration interactively:

    sudo dpkg -i mcprack_*.deb

The installer will ask for:
- Vaultwarden (Bitwarden) server URL, client ID, client secret, master password
- Whether to enable LDAP authentication (optional, disabled by default)
- If LDAP enabled: server URL, base DN, bind DN, bind password
- Whether to enable the FastMCP HTTP proxy (optional, for remote client access)
- If proxy enabled: listen address (127.0.0.1, 0.0.0.0, or custom IP) and port

All configuration is saved to `/etc/mcprack/env` and (if enabled) `/etc/mcprack/proxy.env`.
Database migrations and initial setup run automatically.

## Manual Configuration (if not using interactive install)

Configuration lives in `/etc/mcprack/env`. Required variables:

    SECRET_KEY=<random-string>
    SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI=sqlite:////var/lib/mcprack/mcprack.db
    # or postgresql+psycopg2://... / mysql+pymysql://... (install python3-psycopg2
    # or python3-pymysql respectively — see Suggests in debian/control)

### Authentication (optional, LDAP disabled by default)

    LDAP_ENABLED=false
    # To enable LDAP:
    LDAP_ENABLED=true
    LDAP_SERVER=ldap://10.11.25.3:389
    LDAP_BASE_DN=dc=spojent,dc=cz
    LDAP_USER_OU=ou=Users,dc=spojent,dc=cz
    LDAP_USER_FILTER=(sAMAccountName=%(user)s)
    LDAP_BIND_DN=cn=admin,dc=spojent,dc=cz
    LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD=...

### Vaultwarden (Bitwarden) Credentials

    BW_SERVER=https://vaultwarden-dev.proxy.spojenet.cz
    BW_CLIENTID=...
    BW_CLIENTSECRET=...
    BW_PASSWORD=...
    BITWARDENCLI_APPDATA_DIR=/var/lib/mcprack/.bw

Get `BW_CLIENTID`/`BW_CLIENTSECRET` from the Vaultwarden web vault: Account
Settings → Security → Keys (API Key). `BW_PASSWORD` is that same account's
master password. After setting these, log in to mcprack as an admin and open
"Vaultwarden diagnostics" in the nav bar (`/admin/vaultwarden/wizard`) — it
checks the `bw` binary, `BW_SERVER` reachability, the API key, and the master
password one at a time and tells you exactly which one is still wrong,
instead of a raw "bw login failed" error. Re-run it after every edit to
`/etc/mcprack/env`; no service restart is needed.

### Manual Setup Steps (if needed)

After creating `/etc/mcprack/env`, run database migrations and create admin account:

    su -s /bin/sh mcprack -c 'cd /usr/lib/mcprack && FLASK_APP=app:create_app python3 -c "
from app import create_app
from models import User
from extensions import db
app = create_app()
with app.app_context():
    admin = User(username=\"admin\", auth_type=\"local\", is_admin=True)
    admin.is_active_flag = True
    admin.set_password(\"mcprack-test-2026\")
    db.session.add(admin)
    db.session.commit()
    print(\"Admin account created: admin/mcprack-test-2026\")
"'

Then start the service:

    systemctl enable --now mcprack.service


## MCP HTTP Proxy (for remote access to stdio servers)

If you want remote clients (e.g., Claude Desktop running on a different
workstation) to connect to stdio-based MCP servers running on this host, you
can optionally enable the HTTP proxy during installation.

### During Installation

When you run `sudo dpkg -i`, the installer will ask:
- "Enable FastMCP HTTP proxy? (y/N)"
- If yes: proxy listen address (127.0.0.1, 0.0.0.0, or custom IP)
- If yes: proxy listen port (default 3100)

If you choose `0.0.0.0`, make sure to configure your firewall (firewall004)
to allow external access on the proxy port.

### Manual Configuration (if needed)

Edit `/etc/mcprack/proxy.env`:

    FASTMCP_HOST=127.0.0.1    # or 0.0.0.0 for remote access
    FASTMCP_PORT=3100

Enable and start the proxy service:

    systemctl enable --now mcprack-proxy.service

The proxy will:
- Read all enabled stdio servers from the database
- Generate `/var/lib/mcprack/proxy-mcp.json`
- Start fastmcp on the configured host/port to expose servers over HTTP

When users download their config (e.g., Claude or Copilot), they'll
automatically get network entries pointing to `http://YOUR-HOST:3100/mcp/`
instead of local stdio commands.

To restart the proxy after adding/removing servers:

    systemctl restart mcprack-proxy.service

Note: The proxy port must be accessible from client workstations. Make
sure your firewall allows incoming connections on that port.
